The response is fully buffered before the authoritative size check. A user-controlled PDS can omit or falsify `Content-Length` and stream an arbitrarily large response, potentially exhausting server memory. The reader must consume and cancel the response stream at the byte limit.

2. `src/utils/standard-site-reader.server.ts:167-173` — severity 3, confidence 0.98

```ts
  const url = new URL("/xrpc/com.atproto.repo.listRecords", pds);
  url.searchParams.set("repo", did);
  url.searchParams.set("collection", STANDARD_SITE_DOCUMENT_COLLECTION);
  url.searchParams.set("limit", "100");
  url.searchParams.set("reverse", "true");
  const envelope = recordObject(await fetchJson(url, fetcher));
  const records = Array.isArray(envelope?.records) ? envelope.records : [];
```

`fetchJson` applies the 1,050,000-byte `MAX_RECORD_BYTES` cap to this entire 100-record list response. Standard.site document values include full text and Markdown, so a publication with only a handful of substantial pieces can exceed 1 MiB; the loader throws and the route presents the whole publication as a 404. Listing must use smaller cursor pages or a separate aggregate-response limit.

3. `convex/integrations.ts:477-502` — severity 3, confidence 0.99

```ts
    for (const folio of (foliosRow?.folios ?? []).slice(
      0,
      MAX_FOLIOS_PER_ACCOUNT,
    )) {
      const content = await ctx.db
        .query("folioContent")
        .withIndex("by_userId_folioId", (q) =>
          q.eq("userId", userId).eq("folioId", folio.id),
        )
        .first();
      const text = (content?.html ?? "")
        .replace(/<[^>]+>/g, " ")
        .replace(/\s+/g, " ")
        .trim();
```

Search reads and processes every complete manuscript, up to 500. Convex transactions enforce 16 MiB read and one second of user execution, so established accounts with ordinary manuscript sizes will hit transaction limits and make `folios.search` fail. This needs a Convex search index or bounded/paginated data.

4. `packages/twyne-tools/src/client.ts:68-81` — severity 2, confidence 0.96

```ts
  async request<T>(request: IntegrationRequest, signal?: AbortSignal): Promise<T> {
    let response: Response;
    try {
      response = await this.fetchImpl(this.endpoint, {
        method: "POST",
        headers: {
          authorization: `Bearer ${this.accessToken}`,
          "content-type": "application/json",
          accept: "application/json",
          "user-agent": "@twyne/tools",
        },
        body: JSON.stringify(request),
        signal,
      });
```

There is no default timeout, while all shipped CLI calls omit `signal` and MCP handlers ignore the SDK-provided cancellation signal. A stalled endpoint therefore leaves CLI/MCP calls pending indefinitely, and MCP cancellation cannot abort them. Add a bounded timeout and combine it with the caller/MCP signal.

