Two `fails_silently` findings:

1. `src/utils/standard-site.ts:135-145` — severity 3, confidence 0.97

```ts
  let current: UnknownRecord | null = null;
  try {
    const existing = await agent.com.atproto.repo.getRecord({
      repo: parsed.did,
      collection: STANDARD_SITE_PUBLICATION_COLLECTION,
      rkey: parsed.rkey,
    });
    current = existing.data.value as UnknownRecord;
  } catch {
    // A stale local cache is allowed to recreate its deterministic record.
  }
```

Every `getRecord` error is silently treated as “record absent.” If a transient PDS, authorization, or rate-limit failure is followed by a successful `putRecord`, the publication is rebuilt from `current = null`, discarding existing description, theme, and preferences. Only an explicit not-found response should take this fallback; other failures need reporting/rethrowing. The broad catches in `ensurePublication` at lines 167–192 have the same issue and can silently fall through to duplicate publication creation.

2. `src/routes/at/[did]/[publication]/index.tsx:13-26` and the equivalent document route — severity 3, confidence 0.99

```ts
export const useStandardSitePublication = routeLoader$(
  async ({ params, status }): Promise<LoaderData> => {
    try {
      const did = standardSiteRouteDid(params.did ?? "");
      const page = await loadStandardSitePublication(
        did,
        params.publication ?? "",
      );
      if (!page) status(404);
      return { page };
    } catch {
      status(404);
      return { page: null };
    }
  },
);
```

The loaders swallow every reader failure and report a 404. PDS timeouts/5xx responses, malformed upstream data, and internal bugs are therefore silently presented as “not published,” with no diagnostic for operators. Only actual invalid/not-found cases should become 404; operational failures should be logged and surfaced as unavailable/5xx. The well-known verification handler repeats the same pattern.

